<article class="env-corollary" id="03EV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03EV">Corollary <span data-tag="03EV">7.6.1.26</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits finite products if and only if it satisfies the following pair of conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has a final object ${\bf 1}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits pairwise products. That is, every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ have a product $X \times Y$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The necessity of conditions $(1)$ and $(2)$ is clear (see Example <a data-tag="03EH" href="/tag/03EH">7.6.1.8</a>). Conversely, suppose that $(1)$ and $(2)$ are satisfied, and let $I$ be a finite set. We wish to show that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits $I$-indexed limits. We proceed by induction on the cardinality of $I$. If $I$ is empty, then the desired result follows from assumption $(1)$. If $I$ is a singleton, then the desired result is obvious (see Example <a data-tag="03EJ" href="/tag/03EJ">7.6.1.9</a>). Otherwise, we can write $I$ as a disjoint union of proper subsets $I_{-}, I_{+} \subset I$. Our inductive hypothesis then guarantees that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits $I_{-}$-indexed limits and $I_{+}$-indexed limits. Combining assumption $(2)$ with Corollary <a data-tag="03EU" href="/tag/03EU">7.6.1.25</a>, we deduce that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits limits indexed by $I = I_{-} \coprod I_{+}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
